The themes the app can wear (pick one in the app's Settings, or with
`amarchy theme <name>`), one folder each:

colors.toml
  The theme's palette, exactly as Omarchy ships it (themes/<name>/colors.toml
  at basecamp/omarchy 3c7027d, 2026-10-22). Omarchy is MIT licensed, copyright
  David Heinemeier Hansson: LICENSE-omarchy. Several palettes are Omarchy's
  takes on color schemes of their own (Catppuccin, Everforest, Flexoki,
  Gruvbox, Kanagawa, Nord, Rosé Pine, Tokyo Night), each MIT licensed by its
  authors.

theme.toml
  amarchy's: the theme's name, the style its stand-in wallpaper is drawn in,
  and what Omarchy's hyprland.lua for the theme changes (its border colors,
  rounded corners, how see-through its terminals are).

Omarchy's wallpapers (backgrounds.json)
  The wallpapers the app offers for each theme are Omarchy's own, the
  pictures in themes/<name>/backgrounds/ of basecamp/omarchy. They come
  without a word on who made them or under what license, so they are not in
  this repository and amarchy doesn't redistribute them: each host downloads
  them itself from Omarchy's repository on GitHub (amarchy theme
  --refresh-wallpapers, which install.sh starts) into
  ~/.local/state/amarchy/wallpapers, for its own use. backgrounds.json lists
  only their names, sizes and git hashes, at the commit it names (written by
  backgrounds.py), so every host gets exactly those pictures; the rights to
  them stay with whoever made them.

wallpaper.svg, wallpaper-wide.svg
  amarchy's own, drawn by make.py from the palette, for a phone and for a
  computer's screen: what a theme wears until Omarchy's wallpapers are
  downloaded, or if they can't be. Run it to draw them again.
